1
00:00:00,385 --> 00:00:02,297
In January twenty twenty-one,

2
00:00:02,297 --> 00:00:04,210
some of the most powerful investors on

3
00:00:04,210 --> 00:00:06,260
Wall Street bet that a struggling video

4
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:07,935
game shop would collapse.

5
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:09,798
A man on the internet in a

6
00:00:09,798 --> 00:00:12,635
red headband had bet about fifty-three thousand

7
00:00:12,635 --> 00:00:14,285
dollars that they were wrong.

8
00:00:14,927 --> 00:00:15,852
Within weeks,

9
00:00:15,852 --> 00:00:18,402
his position was worth nearly forty-eight million

10
00:00:18,402 --> 00:00:19,377
dollars.

11
00:00:19,377 --> 00:00:20,914
And one of the funds betting against

12
00:00:20,914 --> 00:00:23,202
him needed billions to keep going.

13
00:00:23,893 --> 00:00:25,593
This is the GameStop story,

14
00:00:25,593 --> 00:00:27,218
explained like you're five.

15
00:00:32,518 --> 00:00:35,368
GameStop sold video games in shopping malls

16
00:00:35,468 --> 00:00:37,881
But people had started downloading games instead

17
00:00:37,881 --> 00:00:39,193
of buying discs.

18
00:00:39,827 --> 00:00:41,839
Then the pandemic shut the malls.

19
00:00:41,839 --> 00:00:43,440
So a lot of big investors looked

20
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:45,365
at GameStop and saw a business that

21
00:00:45,365 --> 00:00:46,377
was dying.

22
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:50,410
When big investors think a company will

23
00:00:50,410 --> 00:00:51,010
fail,

24
00:00:51,010 --> 00:00:52,185
they can bet on it.

25
00:00:52,185 --> 00:00:53,810
It's called short selling.

26
00:00:54,427 --> 00:00:56,527
Imagine you borrow your friend's toy car

27
00:00:56,627 --> 00:00:58,027
It's worth ten dollars.

28
00:00:58,027 --> 00:00:59,227
You sell it straight away,

29
00:00:59,227 --> 00:01:00,502
for ten dollars.

30
00:01:01,152 --> 00:01:01,752
Next week,

31
00:01:01,677 --> 00:01:03,802
toy cars drop to two dollars.

32
00:01:03,802 --> 00:01:05,352
You buy one back for two,

33
00:01:05,352 --> 00:01:06,215
give it to your friend,

34
00:01:06,215 --> 00:01:07,727
and keep eight dollars.

35
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:09,235
That's a short.

36
00:01:09,235 --> 00:01:10,910
You win when the price falls.

37
00:01:11,602 --> 00:01:12,877
But here's the catch.

38
00:01:12,877 --> 00:01:14,627
What if the price goes up instead

39
00:01:14,727 --> 00:01:16,090
You still owe your friend a toy

40
00:01:16,090 --> 00:01:16,690
car.

41
00:01:16,677 --> 00:01:17,840
You have to buy one back,

42
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:19,127
whatever it costs.

43
00:01:19,735 --> 00:01:20,873
When you buy something,

44
00:01:20,873 --> 00:01:22,235
the most you can lose is what

45
00:01:22,235 --> 00:01:23,160
you paid.

46
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:24,223
When you short something,

47
00:01:24,223 --> 00:01:25,385
there's no limit.

48
00:01:27,702 --> 00:01:28,840
With GameStop,

49
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:30,602
so many funds were shorting that something

50
00:01:30,602 --> 00:01:31,902
strange happened.

51
00:01:32,552 --> 00:01:34,490
More shares had been sold short than

52
00:01:34,490 --> 00:01:36,764
were actually available to trade.

53
00:01:36,764 --> 00:01:39,815
America's market regulator later put short interest

54
00:01:39,815 --> 00:01:41,915
at nearly a hundred and twenty-three percent

55
00:01:41,915 --> 00:01:43,527
of the shares available.

56
00:01:44,135 --> 00:01:46,035
It's like borrowing and selling more toy

57
00:01:46,035 --> 00:01:47,485
cars than there are in the whole

58
00:01:47,485 --> 00:01:48,560
playground.

59
00:01:50,852 --> 00:01:52,427
One person had looked at the same

60
00:01:52,427 --> 00:01:54,877
company and seen something different.

61
00:01:55,468 --> 00:01:56,218
Keith Gill,

62
00:01:56,218 --> 00:01:58,193
known online as Roaring Kitty,

63
00:01:58,193 --> 00:02:00,281
believed GameStop was worth far more than

64
00:02:00,281 --> 00:02:01,293
its price.

65
00:02:01,952 --> 00:02:03,052
In twenty nineteen,

66
00:02:03,052 --> 00:02:05,189
he posted his position online.

67
00:02:05,189 --> 00:02:07,402
About fifty-three thousand dollars.

68
00:02:07,402 --> 00:02:08,989
And he kept posting updates,

69
00:02:08,989 --> 00:02:09,990
month after month,

70
00:02:09,990 --> 00:02:12,177
and making videos explaining why.

71
00:02:12,768 --> 00:02:14,705
And people started to notice the trap

72
00:02:14,705 --> 00:02:17,018
the short sellers had built for themselves

73
00:02:19,435 --> 00:02:21,110
If the price started rising,

74
00:02:21,110 --> 00:02:22,735
the short sellers would have to buy

75
00:02:22,735 --> 00:02:24,960
shares back to stop their losses.

76
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:26,710
And buying pushes the price up even

77
00:02:26,710 --> 00:02:27,485
more.

78
00:02:28,102 --> 00:02:30,277
Which forces more short sellers to buy

79
00:02:30,402 --> 00:02:32,252
Which pushes the price higher still.

80
00:02:32,252 --> 00:02:33,802
That's a short squeeze.

81
00:02:34,552 --> 00:02:36,415
In January twenty twenty-one,

82
00:02:36,415 --> 00:02:38,714
huge numbers of ordinary people,

83
00:02:38,714 --> 00:02:41,477
many from an online forum called WallStreetBets

84
00:02:41,577 --> 00:02:42,627
piled in.

85
00:02:43,260 --> 00:02:44,760
On January eleventh,

86
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:46,885
a GameStop share closed at about twenty

87
00:02:46,885 --> 00:02:47,822
dollars.

88
00:02:47,822 --> 00:02:49,585
By January twenty-seventh,

89
00:02:49,585 --> 00:02:51,985
it closed at three hundred and forty-seven

90
00:02:52,702 --> 00:02:53,627
The next morning,

91
00:02:53,627 --> 00:02:56,027
it touched four hundred and eighty-three dollars

92
00:02:56,960 --> 00:02:58,035
One of the funds caught on the

93
00:02:58,035 --> 00:03:00,810
wrong side was called Melvin Capital.

94
00:03:01,452 --> 00:03:03,102
Melvin lost more than half its money

95
00:03:03,102 --> 00:03:04,352
in January.

96
00:03:04,352 --> 00:03:06,377
It took two point seven five billion

97
00:03:06,377 --> 00:03:08,327
dollars from two other firms,

98
00:03:08,327 --> 00:03:10,102
Citadel and Point72,

99
00:03:10,102 --> 00:03:11,302
to keep going.

100
00:03:11,952 --> 00:03:13,915
Short sellers as a group lost billions

101
00:03:13,915 --> 00:03:15,827
of dollars in a matter of days

102
00:03:18,285 --> 00:03:18,885
Then,

103
00:03:18,597 --> 00:03:20,197
on January twenty-eighth,

104
00:03:20,197 --> 00:03:22,610
something happened that made people furious.

105
00:03:23,277 --> 00:03:23,877
Robinhood,

106
00:03:23,839 --> 00:03:25,177
the trading app many of the new

107
00:03:25,177 --> 00:03:26,315
investors used,

108
00:03:26,315 --> 00:03:28,815
stopped customers from buying GameStop.

109
00:03:28,815 --> 00:03:30,527
Other brokers did the same.

110
00:03:30,527 --> 00:03:31,477
You could sell.

111
00:03:31,477 --> 00:03:32,802
You just couldn't buy.

112
00:03:33,402 --> 00:03:34,552
The price dropped.

113
00:03:34,552 --> 00:03:36,677
People accused the brokers of protecting Wall

114
00:03:36,677 --> 00:03:37,427
Street.

115
00:03:38,060 --> 00:03:39,373
The brokers said it was about the

116
00:03:39,373 --> 00:03:40,198
plumbing.

117
00:03:40,198 --> 00:03:41,085
To trade,

118
00:03:41,085 --> 00:03:42,297
they have to put up cash with

119
00:03:42,297 --> 00:03:43,485
a clearing house,

120
00:03:43,485 --> 00:03:45,260
and the wild swings meant they suddenly

121
00:03:45,260 --> 00:03:46,910
had to put up far more.

122
00:03:47,502 --> 00:03:48,965
Congress held hearings.

123
00:03:48,965 --> 00:03:51,127
Regulators wrote a long report.

124
00:03:51,127 --> 00:03:52,415
But in those wild days,

125
00:03:52,415 --> 00:03:54,002
the buy button was gone.

126
00:03:56,318 --> 00:03:57,368
So who won?

127
00:03:58,035 --> 00:03:58,785
At its peak,

128
00:03:58,785 --> 00:04:01,160
Keith Gill's position was worth nearly forty-eight

129
00:04:01,160 --> 00:04:02,185
million dollars,

130
00:04:02,185 --> 00:04:03,235
on paper.

131
00:04:03,885 --> 00:04:06,235
Some people who bought early made fortunes

132
00:04:06,372 --> 00:04:07,735
Many who bought near the top,

133
00:04:07,735 --> 00:04:09,648
at three or four hundred dollars,

134
00:04:09,648 --> 00:04:11,085
lost a lot when the price fell

135
00:04:11,085 --> 00:04:11,835
back.

136
00:04:12,485 --> 00:04:15,210
Melvin Capital closed down in twenty twenty-two

137
00:04:16,127 --> 00:04:17,765
The lesson isn't that the little guy

138
00:04:17,765 --> 00:04:19,039
always wins.

139
00:04:19,039 --> 00:04:19,814
Most of the time,

140
00:04:19,814 --> 00:04:20,677
he doesn't.

141
00:04:21,268 --> 00:04:22,168
It's this.

142
00:04:22,168 --> 00:04:23,368
When you short a stock,

143
00:04:23,368 --> 00:04:24,893
you can lose more than everything you

144
00:04:24,893 --> 00:04:25,768
put in.

145
00:04:25,768 --> 00:04:27,181
And when too many people make the

146
00:04:27,181 --> 00:04:27,968
same bet,

147
00:04:27,968 --> 00:04:29,918
the exit gets very small.

148
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:32,585
Some of Wall Street's biggest players bet

149
00:04:32,585 --> 00:04:34,285
a dying video game shop would go

150
00:04:34,285 --> 00:04:35,335
to zero.

151
00:04:35,335 --> 00:04:36,935
The bet nearly sent one of them

152
00:04:36,935 --> 00:04:38,085
there instead.

